--TEST--
`@expr` is `E0236` in all four positions PHP puts it, and reports exactly once each because the operand takes its place — `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`, `rule:core-api/removals`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/escalation-ladder` makes every runtime failure a `Throwable` propagated by checked
// return (`rule:errors/propagation`), never a diagnostic printed beside a value — so there
// is no channel for `@` to mute, and `rule:core-api/removals` already lists it among
// what that decision closes. The refusal is the parser's, which is why it
// reaches every position alike rather than only the ones the checker would
// have got as far as typing.
array<string> $m = ["k" => "v"];
string $missing = @$m["nope"] ?? "d";

class Risky {
    public static function read(string $k): string {
        return $k;
    }
}

string $got = @Risky::read("x");

int $n = 7;
int $doubled = @$n * 2;

string $tail = @$got . "!";

// Four `@`s, four diagnostics: the count is the assertion. The parser hands
// the operand back in place of the whole `@expr`, so each of these bindings
// is still typed against exactly what it was written to hold and none of
// them reports a second time about a value the suppression swallowed.
echo $missing, $got, $doubled, $tail;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0236]: `@expr` error suppression does not exist
  --> %s:9:19
  |
9 | string $missing = @$m["nope"] ?? "d";
  |                   ^^^^^^^^^^^ there is nothing here to suppress
  = help: a failure is a `Throwable` propagated by checked return, not a diagnostic printed beside a value — catch it with `try`/`catch`

